TOTK: a mesh shader's per-primitive outputs read where the guest code writes them, and handed on as plain vertex outputs
(docs/oot-xlate.md). The attribute area holds every vertex block first (32 vertices, a word per vertex component) and then
every primitive block (32 primitives, a word per per-primitive component), from the end of the last vertex block
(max_vertices / 32 blocks, rounded up); the entry point read them interleaved, each block's per-primitive words after its
vertex words, so a mesh shader of 128 vertices and 64 primitives with four per-primitive components (three of agl's) took
every vertex from 32 on and every per-primitive word from other words. MeshStage.VertexBlockWords, PrimitiveBlockWords and
PrimitiveAreaWord give the layout to both entry points. The per-primitive attributes went out decorated PerPrimitiveEXT,
which the fragment shader's inputs must carry too and do not (its translation does not know its mesh shader): each
primitive now gets vertices of its own that carry its per-primitive words as plain outputs
(MeshStage.PrimitiveOutputsPerVertex, where 256 vertices hold them). The mesh emulation (MeshStage.AsCompute) takes such
shaders too, each triangle on three vertices of its own in the emulation buffer (MeshStage.EmuMeshletVertices,
EmuLocations). A mesh shader without per-primitive outputs translates as before, byte for byte.

